          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em
              Manifold
              = succeed "-diagrams" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            generalize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagrams
            to higher dimensions—they recover string diagrams in dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface+diagram"} surface diagrams
          %p
            The idea of manifold diagrams has its roots in the relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space"} stratified
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifolds
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categories
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagram"} pasting diagrams of directed cells
            in higher categories.
          %p
            A well-known instance of this relation materializes in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+tangle+hypothesis"} generalized tangle hypothesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangle"} tangles
            can (in a canonical way) be regarded as examples of manifold diagrams.
          %center
            %figure
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/some_tangles_Baez_Dolan.png", :style => "width:90%; max-width: 500px; height:auto;"}/
              %figcaption
                Figure 1: m-Tangles in dimension n can be regarded as manifold diagrams (picture reproduces part of Fig. 30 in
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a{:href => "#BaezDolan95"} BaezDolan95
          %p
            From the perspective of the tangle hypothesis, manifold diagrams can be used to express the structure of coherently dualizable objects. More precisely, this translates between “manifold singularities” and relations satisfied by the higher morphisms associated to a coherently dualizable objects. For instance, the manifold diagram in Figure 2 expresses the “swallowtail identity” satisfied by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biadjunction"} coherent biadjunctions
            (this is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %em manifold 4-diagram
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/SwallowtailCatastrophe.html"} this picture
          %center
            %figure
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/swallow_tail_MSRI_cropped.jpeg", :style => "width:90%; max-width: 500px; height:auto;"}/
              %figcaption
                Figure 2: The swallowtail identity as a manifold 4-diagram (reproducing the title image of
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.msri.org/programs/323"} MSRI program 323: higher categories and categorification
          %p
            Many other interesting categorical and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher-algebraic identities
            can be expressed in manifold diagrams in a similar way. As an example, the
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yang%E2%80%93Baxter_equation"} Yang–Baxter equation
            (or, relatedly, the Reidemeister moves) can be expressed in manifold 4-diagrams as well, see Figure 3.
          %center
            %figure
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/yang_baxter_movie.png", :style => "width:90%; max-width: 500px; height:auto;"}/
              %figcaption Figure 3: The Yang-Baxter identity as a manifold 4-diagram, illustrated as movie of 1-tangles in dimension 3 read from left to right.
          %p
            In higher dimensions the types of identites become more and more complicated: Figure 4 shows a
            %em manifold 5-diagram
            representing the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://blogs.ams.org/visualinsight/2016/03/15/zamolodchikov-tetrahedron-equation/"} Zamolodchikov tetrahedron equation
            %a{:href => "#Carter"} Carter
            and collaborators.
          %center
            %figure
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/zamolodchikov_tetrahedron.jpg", :style => "width:90%; max-width: 500px; height:auto;"}/
              %figcaption Figure 4: The Zamolodchikov tetrahedron equation as a manifold 5-diagram, illustrated as a movie from left to right (but the movie only has 2 frames).
          %p
            Manifold diagrams are meant to provide a definitional framework for higher algebraic laws such as those above in
            %em all
            dimensions. Moreover, while the above examples only illustrate such laws for tangles (i.e. for embedded ordinary manifolds), in full generality manifold diagrams are diagrams of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space"} stratified
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifolds
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#outline Outline
          %p
            Several definitional variants exist in dimensions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq 4
            (see also at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface+diagram"} surface diagrams
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#DornDouglas22"} Dorn-Douglas 2022
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+topological+space"} directed euclidean space
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                manifold
                = succeed "-diagram" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is a conical, compactly triangulable stratification of standard
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Here, “standard directed space” can be taken to mean
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+topological+space#framed_spaces"}
                standard
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
                with standard
                = succeed "-framing" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space#conical_strat"} conicality
          %h3#details Details
          %p We provide details for the preceding definition.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em
                standard
                = succeed "-framing" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              is the chain of oriented
              = succeed "-fiber" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i : \mathbb{R}^i \to \mathbb{R}^{i-1}
              (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq i \leq n
              ) with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i
              defined to be the map that forgets the last coordinate of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^i
              (and fibers carry the standard orientation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              after identifying
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^i = \mathbb{R}^{i-1} \times \mathbb{R}
          %p
            When considering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            we tacitly always think of it as ‘standard framed
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            = succeed "-framing" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p#framed_map_euclidean_case
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em framed map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^n
              is a map for which there exist (necessarily unique) maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi j
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_j : \mathbb{R}^j \to \mathbb{R}^j
              (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi j
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq j \leq n
              ) with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n = F
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i \circ F_i = F_{i-1} \circ \pi_i
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_i
              preserving orientations of fibers of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i
              (i.e. mapping fibers strictly monotously).
          %p
            A framed
            %em stratified
            map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R}^n,f) \to (\mathbb{R},g)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space#the_category_of_stratifications"} stratified map
            whose underlying map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^n
            is framed. Moreover, when working with products
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R}^k,f) \times (\mathbb{R}^{n-k}, g)
            we will identitify
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi k
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \cong \mathbb{R}^k \times \mathbb{R}^{n-k}
            in the standard way; and, when working with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space#conical_strat"} cone stratifications
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cone
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} cone
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathrm{Cone}(S^{n-1}), \mathrm{cone}(l))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} into
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n-1} \into \mathbb{R}^n
            and identify
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cone
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Cone}(S^{n-1}) \cong \mathbb{R}^n
            by mapping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \in S^{n-1},\lambda \in [0,1))
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mi λ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo −
                        %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\lambda}{1 - \lambda}x \in \mathbb{R}^n
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A stratification
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R}^n,f)
              is
              %em framed conical
              if each point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \mathbb{R}^n
              it has a framed stratified neighborhood (framed) homeomorphic to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi k
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cone
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} cone
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^k \times (\mathrm{Cone}(S^{n-k-1}), \mathrm{cone}(l))
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi k
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \mathbb{R}^k \times \{0\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq k \leq n
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S^{n-1}, l)
              is some stratification.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em compactly-described triangulation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              is a finite stratification of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              by open disks whose closures are the images of
              %a{:href => "https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/1326711/what-is-a-linear-embedding-from-a-simplex-deltan-to-mathbbrn"} linear embeddings
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi k
                    %mo ×
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %mi l
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} into
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^k \times \mathbb{R}^l_{\geq 0} \into \mathbb{R}^n
              (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mi l
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k + l \leq n
              ). This now translates to the framed stratified case as follows: a stratification
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R}^n,f)
              is
              %em framed compactly triangulable
              if it admits a framed stratified subdivision
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R}^n,K) \to (\mathbb{R}^n,f)
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              by a compactly-described triangulation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p We can now put these concepts together to obtain the following form definition of manifold diagrams.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              (Formalization of earlier sketch). A
              %strong
                manifold
                = succeed "-diagram" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is a framed conical, framed compactly triangulable stratification of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          %p The natural ‘isomorphism’ relation of manifold diagrams is framed stratified homeomorphism.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The definition has several important consequences, which we list in this section. These consequences have been formally worked out in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DornDouglas22"} Dorn and Douglas 22
          %h3#combinatorialization_theorem Combinatorialization theorem
          %p
            While the compact triangulability condition requires the existence of
            %em some
            combinatorial representation, there is in fact a
            %em canonical
            combinatorial representation for isomorphism classes of manifold diagrams. This leads to a theory of combinatorial objects called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss"} trusses
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss#manifold_diagram_classification"} here
          %h3#framed_smooth_structure_of_strata Framed smooth structure of strata
          %p Strata in manifold diagrams are, indeed, manifolds. Moreover, they have canonical smooth structures. This follows since manifold strata carry canonical PL structure, and are canonically framed, and since framed PL manifolds have unique compatible framed smooth structure.
          %h3#welldefinedness_of_links Well-definedness of links
          %p Links of tubular neighborhoods in manifold diagrams are in fact well-defined. That is, there is, up to isomorphism, a unique choice of link. This stands in contrast to classical topological conical stratifications, where non-homeomorphic choices of links exist.
          %h3#dual_cellular_theory_of_manifold_diagrams Dual cellular theory of manifold diagrams
          %p
            Manifold diagrams have canonical geometric duals (in the sense of Poincaré duality). This relates manifold diagrams to ‘diagrams of directed cells’ in the sense of classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagram"} pasting diagrams
            (but with cell shapes that generalize many common classes of shapes such as ‘globular’, ‘simplicial’, or ‘opetopic’ shapes). Details are spelled out in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DornDouglas22"} Dorn and Douglas 22, Sec. 2.4
